{"title":"2017 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Auxiliary_Line)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Auxiliary Line)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Joy has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> thin rods, one each of every integer length from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cm through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/651ba418a810d971dbc8a326d49a71fe1541fae0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> cm. She places the rods with lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cm, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cm, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> cm on a table. She then wants to choose a fourth rod that she can put with these three to form a quadrilateral with positive area. How many of the remaining rods can she choose as the fourth rod?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/7/697d6dc64346b98f9830d6d95a8ca3617351211d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 16\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 17\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 18\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 19\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"394\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The triangle inequality generalizes to all polygons, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/b/5abb2109b354c763cf1bacb02c2bd07c7d7d0aac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 3+7+15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"115\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5ad034548856bfe4f2af12e1e1e429e5576981bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15&lt;x+3+7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"115\" height=\"14\" /> yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/8/a38176c8505a827c3dab192a81c22899ee9f7425.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5&lt;x&lt;25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"88\" height=\"15\" />. Now, we know that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/b/efbfe273946ff9f971c5eab331ca39caa6fdf7f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> numbers between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> exclusive, but we must subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to account for the 2 lengths already used that are between those numbers, which gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c631df4761c074815acc390250af98f167b4c05f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19-2=\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\ 17}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"137\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Auxiliary_Line.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Auxiliary_Line)\">Solution 2 (Auxiliary Line)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Auxiliary Line)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If you do not know the fact that the Triangle Inequality applies to all polygons, you can can solve it completely using the standard triangle inequality that you already know by simply splitting the quadrilateral into two triangles.  Let the four sides of our quadrilateral be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. Draw an auxiliary diagonal line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> that splits the shape into a small triangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and a larger triangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Applying the standard triangle inequality to the first triangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/f/a5fa17b87ef67976b3745dfbe28cd5f1912a5ef3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 - 3 &lt; y &lt; 7 + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"141\" height=\"17\" />, which simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/2/4022585af8ab336079391f58cadad53fc5161326.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 &lt; y &lt; 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"17\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is an imaginary line segment slicing through continuous space, meaning it can be any real decimal value in this interval and does not need to be strictly integers.\n</p><p>Next, we apply the triangle inequality to the second triangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> to determine the boundaries of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. For the upper bound, the unknown side must satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/3/593e1a4a0866b88371d68d92abce47f416db80d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 15 + y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" />. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> maximizes and approaches but never reaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, the absolute upper bound approaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/3/433a2d00206e4a772870528746f50abb1f98bf54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 + 10 = 25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />, meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/2/7c2a9221d5303585bf2da2bdba45f08d356bd75e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &lt; 25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />. For the lower bound, the side of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> must satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/0111c5c9d2328378d4bbeed0217f78421568e506.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 &lt; x + y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" />, which reshapes into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/d/21d7d428553c2ab2e6387f49b4ba3e64d05fbbf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; 15 - y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" />. To minimize <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, we subtract the maximum possible upper limit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> (which approaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />), giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/a/d1af3a6ef0dda90fdb0530f9857439f81c7693f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; 15 - 10 = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"128\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Combining both boundaries gives us the range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/7/287e50cc4fb2403e721e57c4c4876181c61f1ed1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 &lt; x &lt; 25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"88\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> must be an integer, the valid values are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e290f2cba0ba0e11c0b91630da15a408da2fcf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> inclusive. The total count of integers in this pool is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/f/e9f8d2e9a74c3f3dc3f334df34d5b843a62fd978.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24 - 6 + 1 = 19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" />. However, the question states that the rods must be chosen from the remaining pile. The rods of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/f/5ef65994819018f68c862d01bdff5232ae17d51d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7\\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/3/4e3dd5df5a28a11d9fdad92c0d7677f6829e09eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15\\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" /> are already placed on the table to build the other sides of the quadrilateral, so they are unavailable to be chosen again. Subtracting these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> unavailable choices yields our final total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/f/32fcc4deb3baa99bd848210f55746d61628f5a8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19 - 2 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\ 17}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"139\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User%EF%BC%9AIcosahedron0288#Created_Pages\" title=\"User：Icosahedron0288\">Icosahedron0288</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/pxg7CroAt20\">https://youtu.be/pxg7CroAt20</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/RFClyXKH49g\">https://youtu.be/RFClyXKH49g</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A\" title=\"2017 AMC 10A\">2017 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2017 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2017 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2017 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 9\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2017 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2017 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_AMC_10A_Problems/Proble